{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And a man of the house of Levi went and took a daughter of Levi.</b> Rashi explained: he had separated from her, etc. And it appears that he learned this from what is said, 'וַיֵּלֶךְ אִישׁ' — and it does not specify from where he went and to where he went; also, 'מִבֵּית לֵוִי' — it should have said 'וַיֵּלֶךְ אִישׁ לֵוִי.' Rather, since we find the language of 'going' used for divorce, as it says (Deuteronomy 24:2), 'וְהָלְכָה וְהָיְתָה לְאִישׁ אַחֵר,' and the woman is called 'בַּיִת' ('house'), and 'לֵוִי' is an expression of a man's joining to a woman, as in 'הַפַּעַם יִלָּוֶה אִישִׁי אֵלַי' (Genesis 29:34) — therefore it says 'וַיֵּלֶךְ אִישׁ מִבֵּית לֵוִי,' that he went from his house, that is, his wife, and from the bond that had been joined to her. And afterward he returned, 'וַיִּקַּח אֶת בַּת לֵוִי' — he performed a second act of taking with her, for this time her first husband would be joined to her; this is what it says, 'אֶת בַּת לֵוִי.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:53.511370155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}